# Implementation Boundaries

Follow `adaptive_ai_learning_system_v1_2_architecture.pdf` and existing service interfaces. Extend the current design; do not rewrite it unless the developer asks.

## Agent may implement (boilerplate)

- SQLAlchemy models, Alembic migrations, repositories, seed scripts
- Postgres service implementations behind existing interfaces
- Repetitive wiring, exports, imports, and file scaffolding
- Tool wrappers that call existing services without changing policy
- Smoke tests, migration checks, and small verification scripts
- README/docs updates when explicitly requested
- Bug fixes that are clearly local and do not change architecture

## Developer owns (stop and ask first)

- Graph node boundaries, routing policy, and conditional edges
- LangGraph state shape, checkpointer strategy, and session design
- Mastery/confidence formulas, unknown-vs-zero semantics, and update rules
- Prompt design, evaluator behavior, and LLM tool allow-lists
- Concept resolution policy (`find_concept`, lazy creation, de-duplication)
- Security, auth, secrets handling, and production deployment choices
- Phase scope changes or skipping ahead in the roadmap

If a task touches any item above, explain options briefly and wait for approval.

## Hard rules

- Nodes and tools must call services, never SQLAlchemy models directly
- LLM tools are read-only unless the developer explicitly approves writes
- Missing mastery row means unknown (`is_unknown=True`), not zero
- Do not delete hardcoded services until Postgres replacements are tested
- Do not modify unrelated files; keep diffs minimal
- Do not commit, push, or change git config unless explicitly asked

## Before finishing

- Run relevant tests or smoke checks when behavior changed
- Inspect migrations before applying them
- Report assumptions, files changed, and anything left for the developer
- If uncertain, ask instead of guessing
